 "name": "Motion picture, video and television programme production, sound recording and music publishing activities",
 "section": "J",
 "summary": "Film, television and music companies write about generative AI mainly as a rights question: unlicensed training on films, scripts and recordings, AI-generated music and video competing with human work, and digital replicas of performers' voices and likenesses, with guild agreements and litigation shaping what is allowed. They also describe AI in production and post-production (visual effects, de-aging, dubbing, localisation, editing), in catalogue marketing and recommendation, and in new licensing revenue from AI developers. Cinemas mention dynamic pricing and demand forecasting.",
